.TH HI 1 "2026-09-07" "say-hi" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hi \- your shell config, on every host you say hi to
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hi
.RI [ ssh\-options ]
.RB [ \-\-use " " \fIbackend\fR]
.RB [ \-\-plain ]
.RB [ \-\-mux | \-\-no\-mux ]
.I target
.RI [ command ...]
.br
.B hi
.br
.B hi \-\-doctor
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-\-use " " \fIbackend\fR]
.RI [ target ]
.br
.B hi \-\-install
.RB [ \-\-yes ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-link ]
.RB [ \-\-system\-link ]
.RB [ \-\-preset " " \fIname\fR]
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.br
.B hi \-\-configure
.RB [ \-\-preset " " \fIname\fR]
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.br
.B hi \-\-uninstall
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.br
.B hi \-\-update
.RI [ tag ]
.br
.B hi \-\-preview
.RB { colors | packages | header }
.br
.BR "hi \-h" " | " \-V
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hi
copies your
.B say-hi
configuration tree (prompt, colors, aliases, vim/nano configs, connect
header) to a target, starts a session there, and removes every trace when
the session ends. It is sshrc, supercharged.
.PP
With no
.IR command ,
.B hi
opens an interactive session on the target (see
.B SESSION SHELL
for which shell). With a trailing
.IR command ,
it runs that command on the target and exits \- inside hi's session, not the
way
.BR ssh (1)
runs one: the command has hi's aliases, PATH and environment, and runs on a
pty when your own stdin is one. Only the command's own output goes to stdout;
hi's progress and errors go to stderr. For a plain, unstyled, pty\-free
remote command \- a tarball you are piping into a file, say \- use
.BR ssh (1)
itself.
.PP
The payload is base64\-armored through the target's login shell for ssh
targets, unpacked into a private
.BR mktemp (1)
directory, and removed by cleanup traps on exit \- including on an abrupt
disconnect. A say-hi installed on the target itself is neither read nor
touched by a session: it is there for that machine's own shells.
.SH SESSION SHELL
The session runs in your login shell when hi styles it (bash, zsh or fish),
otherwise in the best of fish > zsh > bash. Both halves come off one
ordering \- fish > zsh > bash > dash > ash > sh \- so a target with no bash
at all falls back to the best of the rest of it: fish > zsh > dash > ash > sh,
with hi's aliases and colored prompt but no header.
.SH TARGET RESOLUTION
.I target
is tried against each backend, in order; the first match wins:
.TP
.B 1. ssh host
a literal
.B Host
entry in
.B ~/.ssh/config
(wildcard entries are not matched).
.TP
.B 2. container, through docker, podman, nerdctl or finch
a running container, by name or ID, on whichever of the four
answers, in that order.
.TP
.B 3. nomad allocation
a running allocation, by ID or ID prefix.
.TP
.B 4. kubernetes pod
a running pod in the current
.BR kubectl (1)
context and namespace, or
.I namespace:pod
and
.I context:namespace:pod
for one elsewhere \- the prefixes become
.B kubectl \-\-context
and
.BR "\-\-namespace" .
Completion spells a pod outside the current namespace the same way.
.PP
The last two can hold more than one thing to land in, and a
.B /
suffix says which:
.I alloc/task
becomes
.B nomad alloc exec \-task
and
.I pod/container
becomes
.BR "kubectl exec \-c" .
Without it the two differ:
.BR nomad (1)
refuses a multi-task exec outright, while
.BR kubectl (1)
falls back to the pod's first container and warns. Completion offers the
pairs for any allocation or pod that has more than one.
.PP
A name matching none of these is handed to
.BR ssh (1)
anyway, so unlisted hosts still work.
.PP
.BR \-\-use " " \fIbackend\fR
before
.I target
names the arm outright and skips every probe above it \- the fix for a
container that shadows an unrelated ssh host of the same name, or the other
way around.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-h\fR, \fB\-\-help
print a usage summary \- the synopsis, the target resolution order, and hi's
own options \- and exit. Everything here in short form; this page is the long
one. Caught anywhere ahead of the target, so it never reaches
.BR ssh (1).
Takes nothing after it; a stray word is refused.
.TP
.B \-V\fR, \fB\-\-version
print the version and exit: the version stamped in at packaging time, or
.B git describe
output in a checkout, followed by which kind of tree answered (a checkout,
a package, or a bare tree) and where it is. Takes nothing after it. The one short
.BR ssh (1)
option hi claims for itself \- hi's version is the first thing a bug report
asks for, and
.B ssh \-V
is a keystroke away for the other one.
.TP
.B \-\-use \fIbackend\fR
force that arm for
.I target
and skip every probe in
.B TARGET RESOLUTION
above:
.B ssh
(ssh's own error speaks if the host is not there), or a backend by name \-
.BR docker ,
.BR podman ,
.BR nerdctl ,
.BR finch ,
.B nomad
or
.BR kube .
The name has to be one of those; a stranger is an error, not a guess.
.B \-\-use=\fIbackend\fR
is the same flag with its word joined \- as every option that takes one word
is:
.B \-\-preview=colors
too. An option that takes no word refuses a joined one
.RB ( \-\-plain=1 ,
.BR \-\-install=yes ).
.TP
.B \-\-plain
skip the payload entirely and hand over a bare shell on whichever arm
.I target
resolves to \- no copy, no aliases, no header. Needs nothing from the target
beyond a shell (and, for an ssh target,
.BR sshd (8))
\- not even
.BR tar (1)
or
.BR base64 (1),
and no writable
.B /tmp
or
.B $HOME
either.
.TP
.B \-\-mux
run the session inside a local multiplexer session \-
.BR tmux (1),
.BR zellij (1)
or
.BR screen (1),
whichever is first on your PATH \- named for the target
.RB ( hi\-<target> ,
with the characters tmux rejects turned into dashes), so a dropped link leaves
the session running here: the next
.B hi \-\-mux
to the same target reattaches instead of opening a second one. Entirely
client\-side \- the target sees the same disposable session as ever. From
inside a tmux, the session is created detached and this client switches to
it; inside a screen or zellij, a new window or tab opens in the current
session. Without a terminal, or without a multiplexer on this machine, hi
connects as usual.
.B alias hi='hi \-\-mux'
makes it the default.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-mux
connect without the tmux wrap \- the way out of that alias for one connect. Of
.B \-\-mux
and
.BR \-\-no\-mux ,
the last one on the line wins, so the flag on the command line beats the one
the alias put there.
.PP
The local commands act on this machine instead of connecting, and each needs
a part of the tree that is not always present. All of them want
.BR scripts/ ,
which a package\-manager install ships but a hi session does not \- so inside a
session each says so and stops.
.B \-\-update
wants
.B .git
on top of that, and a package carries none.
.TP
.B \-\-preview \fR{\fBcolors\fR|\fBpackages\fR|\fBheader\fR}
print one of three things and exit, instead of connecting.
.B colors
is every ssh host and your user in their resolved colors, each row naming the
rule it matched \- a pin, a tag, a pattern or the hash.
.B packages
is the package\-priority legend, as the header's check prints it, with the
ranks the floor silences marked.
.B header
is the connect header itself, as it prints here at your settings.
.TP
.B \-\-doctor \fR[\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-use\fR \fIbackend\fR] [\fItarget\fR]
print a read\-only pre\-flight report instead of connecting
.RB ( \-\-doctor=\fItarget\fR
is the joined spelling, so a typo there is a target to probe, not an error): the local tree,
the config overlay, the shell rc files and the overlay's own shell files
parsed by whatever will read them (the rc half is what
.B \-\-install
checks before it writes a line), the install itself \- each shell's rc lines
and whether they name this tree, the
.B hi
link and what
.B hi
on
.B PATH
runs, and the file a login bash or a
.B ZDOTDIR
zsh really reads \- every backend probed and timed, and \- with a target \-
which backend the name resolves to plus an ssh reachability check.
.B \-\-use
reports the same forced backend a real connect with it would take, skipping
the probe chain. With
.BR \-\-json ,
the same rows as one JSON document on stdout \- what a bug report should
carry \- with the finding count in it as
.BR findings .
Exits 1 on any finding (see
.BR "EXIT STATUS" ).
.TP
.B \-\-install \fR[\fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-\-link\fR {\fBnone\fR|\fBuser\fR|\fBsystem\fR}] [\fB\-\-preset\fR \fIname\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
install or repair say-hi's lines in your shell rc files (bash, zsh and fish,
each only when it is installed; on macOS a line in
.B ~/.bash_profile
so a login bash reads
.BR ~/.bashrc ),
seed the config overlay at
.B ~/.config/say-hi/
with the shipped defaults (files already there are never touched), link
.B ~/.local/bin/hi
at this tree, and open the settings menu of
.B \-\-configure
at a terminal. Each rc file is syntax\-checked first, and a file that does
not parse stops the install with a question; that is the one prompt, and
.BR \-y / \-\-yes
answers it.
.BR \-n / \-\-dry\-run
is the short form on every local command that has one.
.B \-\-link
says where the
.B hi
link goes:
.B user
(the default,
.BR ~/.local/bin/hi ),
.B system
.RB ( /usr/bin/hi ,
through sudo), or
.B none
(no link at all \- the wired shells alias
.B hi
either way; the link is for scripts and other programs). A link a package
owns is never touched, and
when a
.B hi
already on your
.B PATH
runs this tree (a package's
.BR /usr/bin/hi ,
Homebrew's wrapper) no link is made at all.
.B \-\-preset
.I name
answers the settings without the menu, as under
.BR \-\-configure .
.B \-\-dry\-run
prints every write \- rc lines, the seed, settings.sh, the link \- and makes
none of them. The script behind it,
.BR scripts/install.sh ,
also takes
.BR \-\-features\-only " (what " \-\-configure " runs),"
.B \-\-check\-configs
(the rc syntax check alone, as an exit status) and
.BR \-\-prefix " " \fIdir\fR,
packaging mode \- stage the tree under
.I dir
with
.B /usr/bin/hi
and
.B /etc/profile.d/say\-hi.sh
beside it, and write nothing to a home \- which the package builds use and a
user never needs.
.TP
.B \-\-uninstall \fR[\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
take those lines back out again, remove the settings.sh the install wrote,
and unlink hi where the link points at this tree \-
.B ~/.local/bin/hi
and a
.B /usr/bin/hi
of an earlier
.BR \-\-system\-link ,
never a package's. A file the install created and nothing else wrote into
(a macOS
.BR ~/.bash_profile )
goes with its lines. The tree itself, the rest
of the overlay and the one\-time
.B .hi\-orig
backups are left where they are.
.TP
.B \-\-configure \fR[\fB\-\-preset\fR \fIname\fR]
revisit the settings only, leaving the rc wiring alone: a menu over a live
preview of the header and the prompt. Pick a preset (\fBeverything\fR,
\fBbalanced\fR, \fBminimal\fR), or open a section \- Header (the real header
rendered above a list of its items: toggle, reorder, presets, width, the
package check's depth, the addresses the ip cell hides), Features,
Prompt, Advanced \- then
\fBs\fR saves and \fBq\fR leaves the file untouched; nothing is written
before either.
.B \-\-preset
.I name
applies one without the menu.
.TP
.B \-\-update \fR[\fItag\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
move the say-hi checkout to a release tag: the tags are fetched and the newest
is checked out, detached \- or the
.I tag
named, to move on or back.
.BR \-n / \-\-dry\-run
fetches the tags and names the one it would check out, moving nothing. Releases are tags and nothing else; following a
branch is
.B git pull
in the checkout, by hand.
A working tree with uncommitted changes is refused before anything moves \-
commit or stash first.
Needs a checkout: an install from a package has no
.B .git
to move, and says so rather than running.
.PP
Everything else is passed through to
.BR ssh (1)
unchanged \-
.BR \-p ,
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-J ,
.BR \-o ,
and the rest behave exactly as they do there.
.BR ssh (1)
takes no option that starts with two dashes, so every one of those is hi's:
one it does not know is hi's error, not ssh's usage message. Only the first
non\-option word is taken as the target; everything after it, dashed or not,
is the remote command \- so
.B hi host ls \-la
reads as it does for ssh, and one of hi's own options there is refused by
name rather than run on the far end.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B a connect
the session's, or the remote command's, own exit status \- what
.BR ssh (1)
would hand back \- once the target has been cleaned up. 1 when hi itself
refused before connecting: an option it does not know, one of its own out of
place, a
.B \-\-use
name it was not loaded with, two of them, or a local command run inside a
session. An
.I ssh\-option
with no target hands back what
.BR ssh (1)
exited with.
.TP
.B the target's bootstrap
64 when the target has no
.BR base64 (1)
to unpack the payload with, 65 when it could not make a scratch directory \-
the two ways a target refuses a session before a shell opens, each reported
by hi with the fix.
.TP
.B \-\-doctor
0 with nothing to report, 1 on any finding. The count is in the report
(and in
.BR \-\-json 's
.BR findings ),
never in the status.
.TP
.B the other local commands
the script's own: 0 when it did what it says, 1 otherwise \- an rc file
that fails its syntax check stops
.B \-\-install
at 1 before it writes a line.
.SH SESSION COMMANDS
One command hi's aliases define inside a session (and in the wired local
shells), spelled with an underscore because they are shell functions, not
flags of
.BR hi :
.TP
.B hi_info
print where the running say\-hi lives \- its home, root and launcher. A
diagnostic (the test harness's "the session is up" probe), and not part of
the 1.x contract.
.SH COMPLETION
.B hi
completes in bash, zsh and fish. All three read one list \-
.B common/targets.sh
\- so the three can not drift apart, and it is the only one fish can run.
.TP
.B hi <TAB>
every target: the
.B Host
entries in
.B ~/.ssh/config
plus every running container, allocation and pod, each tagged with the
backend it came from. The list is reused for five seconds and each backend
gets two seconds to answer, because this runs on every keypress.
.TP
.B hi \-\-<TAB>
hi's own options, each with its one\-line purpose where the shell has a
column for it (fish and zsh do; bash lists the names). Answered without
probing any backend at all. The list is
filtered to what this install can actually run: a session is offered none of
the local commands that need
.BR scripts/ ,
and a package\-manager install is not offered
.BR \-\-update ,
which needs
.BR .git .
.TP
.B hi \-\-install \-\-<TAB>
behind a local command, that command's own switches instead \- the ones its
usage line names \- and
.B hi \-\-doctor \-\-use <TAB>
the backends, as
.B \-\-use
completes on a connect;
.B \-\-link
and
.B \-\-preset
their values, and
.B hi \-\-update <TAB>
the release tags a checkout knows of.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Configuration lives outside the tree, in
.B ~/.config/say-hi/
.RB ( settings.sh ,
.BR colors ,
.BR packages ,
.BR vim.rc
and
.BR nano.rc ,
additive
.BR aliases.sh ,
and per-shell
.BR bash.sh ,
.B zsh.zsh
and
.BR config.fish ),
written by
.B hi \-\-configure
and carried along to every target. Every feature is an environment
variable toggle \- header, prompt, git status, editors \- each on
by default and tabulated in
.IR docs/SETTINGS.md .
The tree itself can be
root\-owned (a package\-manager install): nothing hi does writes into it.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /usr/share/say-hi
the tree, for package\-manager installs (a git checkout at
.B ~/say-hi
works identically).
.TP
.B ~/.config/say-hi/settings.sh
feature toggles, written by
.BR "hi \-\-configure" .
.TP
.B ~/.config/say-hi/colors, ~/.config/say-hi/packages
per\-user overrides of the tree's defaults.
.TP
.B ~/.config/say-hi/vim.rc, ~/.config/say-hi/nano.rc
per\-user replacements of the tree's editor configs, used whole rather than
merged.
.TP
.B ~/.config/say-hi/aliases.sh
your own aliases, sourced first so any
.B _HI_*_OPTS
value or
.B _HI_DISABLE_*
toggle you set there wins over the shipped set - an
.B alias
of the same name does not, since the shipped one is defined after and
overwrites it.
.TP
.B ~/.config/say-hi/bash.sh, ~/.config/say-hi/zsh.zsh, ~/.config/say-hi/config.fish
your own per\-shell preferences, sourced after hi's own so yours win.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Every setting is an environment variable, and the full table with defaults is
.IR docs/SETTINGS.md ;
.B hi \-\-configure
writes them to
.IR settings.sh .
The ones that change what a connect does:
.TP
.B _HI_HOME
the parent of the
.B say-hi
directory; set by
.B /etc/profile.d/say-hi.sh
for packaged installs.
.TP
.B _HI_TRUECOLOR
.B 1
or
.BR 0 :
whether the client's terminal renders 24\-bit color; read off
.B COLORTERM
and shipped to the session, which ssh never forwards itself.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B hi prod-db
interactive session on the ssh host
.BR prod\-db ,
with your prompt, colors and aliases.
.TP
.B hi -J bastion internal 'df -h'
run a command on
.B internal
through a jump host.
.TP
.B hi 8f41c2
session inside a running container or allocation with that ID.
.TP
.B hi \-\-use docker web
the container named
.BR web ,
even though an ssh host of the same name exists \- no probe is run.
.TP
.B hi \-\-plain rescue
a bare shell on
.B rescue
with nothing copied: for a box with no writable
.B /tmp
or no
.BR tar (1).
.TP
.B hi \-\-doctor \-\-json prod-db > report.json
the pre\-flight report as a document, for a bug report; the exit status
says whether anything was found.
.TP
.B hi
no target: print this help and exit.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ssh (1)
.PP
Full documentation \- the toggle tables in
.IR docs/SETTINGS.md ,
the testing story in
.IR docs/TESTING.md ,
and the README over both:
.I https://github.com/ivylikethevine/say-hi
